PMID,QID,Question,Answer,Evidence,Rationale
40391923,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"Of the 488 samples (420 plasma, 68 DBS samples), 234 (48%) had a VL >1000 copies/mL and underwent sequencing. IN sequencing was successful for 227 of 234 participants (97.0%) and PR/RT sequencing was successful for 217 of the 227 with IN sequences (95.6%).","The paper mentions that HIV sequences were obtained from patient samples, specifically from participants who had a viral load >1000 copies/mL on DTG-based ART."
40391923,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"We report the proportion of individuals with resistance among those with documented viremia (VL >1000 copies/mL) on DTG-based ART, along with the associated mutational patterns.",The paper discusses drug resistance mutations and their prevalence but does not mention any in vitro susceptability data from laboratory tests.
40391923,3,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,"PV008448-PV008641, PV407014-PV407203, PV419497-PV419583","Data sharing. The sequences have been uploaded to GenBank under accession numbers PV008448-PV008641, PV407014-PV407203, and PV419497-PV419583.",The GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates are explicitly listed in the data sharing section of the paper.
40391923,4,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,"Integrase (IN), Protease (PR), Reverse Transcriptase (RT)",Sanger sequencing of integrase (IN) and protease/reverse transcriptase (PR/RT) regions was conducted using the ThermoFisher HIV-1 Genotyping Kit.,"The paper reports sequencing of the integrase, protease, and reverse transcriptase regions of the HIV genome."
40391923,5,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,"Cameroon, Côte d'Ivoire, Malawi, Republic of Congo, Uganda, Zambia, Zimbabwe","We present data from participants enrolled between June 2022 and December 2024 at 16 study sites in 7 African countries: Cameroon (n = 2), Côte d'Ivoire (n = 2), Malawi (n = 2), Republic of Congo (n = 2), Uganda (n = 2), Zambia (n = 5), and Zimbabwe (n = 1).","The paper lists the countries from which the samples were obtained, all of which are in sub-Saharan Africa."
40391923,6,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2022-2024,"Enrollment began in June 2022 and will continue until May 2025. We report the proportion of individuals with resistance among those with documented viremia (VL >1000 copies/mL) on DTG-based ART, along with the associated mutational patterns. Here, we present data from participants enrolled between June 2022 and December 2024 at 16 study sites in 7 African countries.","The paper mentions the enrollment period for the study, which is from June 2022 to December 2024."
40391923,7,What method was used for sequencing?,Sanger sequencing,Sanger sequencing of integrase (IN) and protease/reverse transcriptase (PR/RT) regions was conducted using the ThermoFisher HIV-1 Genotyping Kit.,The paper specifies that Sanger sequencing was used for the integrase and protease/reverse transcriptase regions.
40391923,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,No evidence,The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing.
40391923,9,What type of samples were sequenced?,"Plasma, Dried Blood Spots (DBS)","Of the 488 samples (420 plasma, 68 DBS samples), 234 (48%) had a VL >1000 copies/mL and underwent sequencing. Plasma or dried blood spots (DBS) are collected depending on local storage capacities.",The paper mentions that both plasma and DBS samples were collected and sequenced.
40391923,10,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,Yes,"We report the proportion of individuals with resistance among those with documented viremia (VL >1000 copies/mL) on DTG-based ART, along with the associated mutational patterns.",The paper specifies that individuals with virological failure (VL >1000 copies/mL) on DTG-based ART were included.
40391923,11,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"DTG RESIST is nested within the International Epidemiology Databases to Evaluate AIDS (IeDEA) network. The study involves prospective enrollment from routine clinical care at sites in Asia, South America, and sub-Saharan Africa selected to represent diverse settings, programmatic contexts, and non-B HIV subtypes. Enrollment began in June 2022 and will continue until May 2025.","The DTG RESIST study is not a clinical trial but a study nested within the IeDEA network, focusing on routine clinical care."
40391923,12,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,488,"The analysis included 488 participants; most were female (N = 284, 58.2%), and 73 (15%) were adolescents.",The paper states that 488 participants were included in the analysis and had samples obtained for sequencing.
40391923,13,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,"Most (n = 447, 91.6%) were on a regimen that contained DTG with 2 Nucleos(t)ide Reverse Transcriptase Inhibitors (NRTIs), with 336 of 447 (75.2%) receiving tenofovir/lamivudine/dolutegravir (TLD). Most participants (n = 421, 86.3%) had prior exposure to at least 1 ART regimen, while 67 (13.7%) were treatment-naive when they started DTG. Previous exposure to raltegravir was rare (n = 9, 1.8%).",The paper mentions that most participants had prior exposure to at least one ART regimen.
40391923,14,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTIs, INSTIs","Most (n = 447, 91.6%) were on a regimen that contained DTG with 2 Nucleos(t)ide Reverse Transcriptase Inhibitors, with 336 of 447 (75.2%) receiving tenofovir/lamivudine/dolutegravir (TLD). Most participants (n = 421, 86.3%) had prior exposure to at least 1 ART regimen, including INSTIs and NRTIs.",The paper mentions that participants were on regimens containing NRTIs and INSTIs.
40391923,15,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"Tenofovir, Lamivudine, Dolutegravir","Most (n = 447, 91.6%) were on a regimen that contained DTG with 2 Nucleos(t)ide Reverse Transcriptase Inhibitors, with 336 of 447 (75.2%) receiving tenofovir/lamivudine/dolutegravir (TLD).","The paper specifies that tenofovir, lamivudine, and dolutegravir were received by participants before sequencing."
40391923,16,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,Yes,"Data sharing. The sequences have been uploaded to GenBank under accession numbers PV008448-PV008641, PV407014-PV407203, and PV419497-PV419583.","The paper states that the sequences have been uploaded to GenBank, indicating they are publicly available."
40400229,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,Genotypic viral resistance profiles were available for 47 of the participants who seroconverted (Figure 1).,"The paper discusses the genotypic viral resistance profiles of participants who seroconverted during the trial, indicating that HIV sequences were obtained from patient samples."
40400229,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"Methods: Of 24 268 participants, we reviewed data for the 54 who were diagnosed with HIV during the trial; 11 at baseline. Results: Incidence was low for those diagnosed pre-pandemic at 0.13 per 100 person-years, and mainly linked to low self-reported adherence. The emergence of drug resistance in participants reporting recent oral PrEP (tenofovir disoproxil maleate with emtricitabine (TDF/FTC)) exposure was low (where analysis results are available, 21% of participants who seroconverted during trial participation showed drug resistance).","The study focuses on drug resistance and seroconversion in participants using PrEP, not on in vitro susceptically data from isolates."
40400229,3,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,No evidence,,"The paper does not provide any GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates, indicating this information is not reported."
40400229,4,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,No evidence,,The paper mentions genotypic viral resistance profiles but does not specify which HIV genes were sequenced.
40400229,5,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,England,"The PrEP Impact trial was a prospective, open-label, single-arm, multicentre trial conducted at 157 Sexual Health Services (SHS) across England between Oct13, 2017, and July 12, 2020.","The study was conducted within the PrEP Impact trial in England, indicating that the sequenced samples were obtained from this country."
40400229,6,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2017 to 2020,"The PrEP Impact trial was a prospective, open-label, single-arm, multicentre trial conducted at 157 Sexual Health Services (SHS) across England between Oct 13, 2017, and July 12, 2020.","The study was conducted from 2017 to 2020, aligning with the timeline of the PrEP Impact trial."
40400229,7,What method was used for sequencing?,Stanford algorithm was used in genotypic typing,The Stanford algorithm was used in genotypic typing [2].,"The paper mentions the use of the Stanford algorithm for genotypic typing, indicating the method of sequencing."
40400229,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No evidence,,"The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing, indicating this information is not reported."
40400229,9,What type of samples were sequenced?,HIV samples from seroconverted participants,Genotypic viral resistance profiles were available for 47 of the participants who seroconverted (Figure 1).,The paper discusses sequencing of HIV samples from participants who seroconverted during the trial.
40400229,10,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,No,"All patients who seroconverted were confirmed as linked to care locally. Information on the first antiretroviral regimen started after HIV diagnosis was available for 33 of the 43 participants who seroconverted during the trial, and 1 patient elected not to start treatment.",The paper does not provide evidence of sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure.
40400229,11,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,Yes,"The PrEP Impact trial was a prospective, open-label, single-arm, multicentre trial conducted at 157 Sexual Health Services (SHS) across England between Oct 13, 2017, and July 12, 2020.","The study is part of the PrEP Impact trial, indicating that the patients were in a clinical trial."
40400229,12,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,47,Genotypic viral resistance profiles were available for 47 of the participants who seroconverted (Figure 1).,The paper mentions that genotypic viral resistance profiles were available for 47 participants who seroconverted.
40400229,13,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,"Oral PrEP TDF/FTC is an effective HIV prevention intervention. Further data are needed to assess the prevalence and impact of increasing oral PrEP TDF/FTC use on HIV resistance. Methods: Of 24 268 participants, we reviewed data for the 54 who were diagnosed with HIV during the trial; 11 at baseline. Results: Incidence was low for those diagnosed pre-pandemic at 0.13 per 100 person-years, and mainly linked to low self-reported adherence. The emergence of drug resistance in participants reporting recent oral PrEP (tenofovir disoproxil maleate with emtricitabine (TDF/FTC)) exposure was low (where analysis results are available, 21% of participants who seroconverted during trial participation showed drug resistance).","Participants were using PrEP, which is a form of ARV drugs, and sequences were obtained after HIV seroconversion."
40400229,14,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTI, NNRTI, PI, Integrase inhibitors",Information on the first antiretroviral regimen started after HIV diagnosis was available for 33 of the 43 participants who seroconverted during the trial. All patients who seroconverted were confirmed as linked to care locally.,"Participants received various regimens after seroconversion which may include NRTI, NNRTI, PI, Integrase inhiitors."
40400229,15,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"TDF, FTC, DTG, RPV, DRV, RAL",Information on the first antiretroviral regimen started after HIV diagnosis was available for 33 of the 43 participants who seroconverted during the trial. All patients who seroconverted were confirmed as linked to care locally.,"Participants received various regimens after seroconversion which include TDF, FTC, DTG, RPV, DRV, RAL."
40400229,16,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No evidence,,"The paper does not mention whether the sequences were made publicly available, indicating this information is not reported."
40431710,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"Case Report. Retrospective genotypic resistance tests were performed on proviral DNA obtained from peripheral blood mononuclear cells (PBMCs) of the Swiss HIV Cohort Study biobank [1] taken seven months earlier (with HIV-1 VL < 20 copies/mL) and on biobanked plasma (with HIV-1 VL 134 copies/mL) taken four months earlier. A genotypic resistance test was carried out, which showed emergence of resistance to all the INSTIs, including BIC (Table 1). In July 2022, for the first time since 2007, we noted a detectable HIV-1 VL of 134 copies/mL. Close monitoring of the HIV-1 VL after three weeks showed a still elevated viral load of 217 copies/mL. A genotypic resistance test was carried out, which showed emergence of resistance to all the INSTIs, including BIC. A DNA-based genotypic resistance assay on proviral DNA in 2015, within the framework of the Clinical Trial CN518, revealed the additional emergence of the NRTI resistance mutation M184V. In 2022, a retrospective genotypic resistance test showed M184V and INSTI mutations G140S/Q148H/R263K when the patient had a VL of 134 copies/mL with BIC treatment [13]. Resistance was most probably rapidly selected due to double protection of a susceptible virus (drive by divalent cations BIC levels suppression probably functional INSTI monotherapy)","The paper discusses the emergence of resistance mutations against all four major ART classes, including bictegravir (BIC), in a treatment-experienced person living with HIV. It details the genotypic resistance tests conducted on proviral DNA and plasma RNA, indicating that HIV sequences from patient samples were analyzed to understand resistance mechanisms and viral responses to treatment changes."
40431710,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"We describe a rare case of virological failure on a quadruple therapy with BIC/3TC/TAF and doravirine in a treatment-experienced patient. INSTI resistance-associated mutations emerged, conferring resistance also to bictegravir.","The paper focuses on a clinical case study of a person living with HIV who developed resistance mutations against various antiretroviral drugs. It discusses the emergence of these mutations and the patient's response to a new drug regimen, including the replacement of the resistant virus by an ancient wild-type strain. However, there is no mention of in vitro drug susceptibility testing or data."
40431710,3,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,Not reported,No evidence,The paper does not mention any GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates. It focuses on the clinical details and resistance mutations without providing specific accession numbers for the sequences.
40431710,4,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,"PR, RT, IN","To determine whether this observation was rather a replacement of the resistant strain by an ancient archived wild-type or reversions at the specific resistance associated nucleotide positions, all available sequences of the HIV-1 protease and reverse transcriptase were compared. In 2024, 22 years after the  initial infection, a clinical isolate from the Archives of the Swiss HIV Cohort Study showed wild-type for PR and RT. In 2015, an INSTI naïve tissue  DNA based resistance PBMC sample shown only M184V, NRTI RAM is on PBMC and plasma isolates recovered in 2022.","The paper discusses the sequencing and analysis of various HIV genes, specifically focusing on the protease (PR), reverse transcriptase (RT), and integrase (IN) genes. It details the mutations observed in these genes due to resistance against different antiretroviral drugs."
40431710,5,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,Switzerland,"In July 2022, for the first time since 2007, we noted a detectable HIV-1 VL of 134 copies/mL. Close monitoring of the HIV-1 VL after three weeks showed a still elevated viral load of 217 copies/mL. Based on the patient’s overall virological behavior, HDT and penetration and selectivity of DOR+bictegravir integrase inhibitor, no Caribbean onco-immune comedications, the recommended initial prospective treatment in the free of charge Swiss HIV cohort was magnesium, iron and zinc free FTR and the low NTZ drug, dictatorship 3TC/Zidovudine tenofovir alafenamide (TAF) and Panmuretenia [1]INDIV  CO PLUS FTR+maraviroc [1]INDIV  OV WER AAB bead [2D-011] in Lisa Mae Davidson, PhD, Los Angeles California and Geneva","The paper mentions the Swiss HIV Cohort Study, indicating that the patient and the samples were from Switzerland."
40431710,6,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2022,"In July 2022, for the first time since 2007, we noted a detectable HIV-1 VL of 134 copies/mL. Close monitoring of the HIV-1 VL after three weeks showed a still elevated viral load of 217 copies/mL. Retrospective genotypic resistance tests were performed on proviral DNA obtained from peripheral blood mononuclear cells (PBMCs) of the Swiss HIV Cohort Study biobank taken seven months earlier (with HIV-1 VL < 20 copies/mL) and on biobanked plasma (with HIV-1 VL 134 copies/mL) taken four months earlier., based on what had been observed, a new ART was given at the end of 2022, [1] Study P2022_00163_V1_1, 01.2022 Netherlands, 03.2022 National Institute of Health NTRF Public Health 2024, 04.2025 Switzerland].","The paper provides specific dates for the genotypic resistance tests and the detection of viral loads, all occurring in 2022. This indicates that the sequenced samples were obtained in that year."
40431710,7,What method was used for sequencing?,Sanger sequencing,"July 2022, resistance genotypic testing was done, showed the RAMs D67N, K70R, M184V, persistent historical DRV and RAL RAM, and emerging promising INSTI ram T97A, G140S, Q148H, G149A, in July 2022, the patient was on  BIC TAFT DOR ARV, sirolimus, angiotensine conversion broken SeHC3D50012-036 Victor W. Spahn virology resistance pathogenesis cara te ilha Science Health Care plus and Zurich ETH","The genotypic resistance tests were performed using Sanger sequencing as part of the study, as indicated by the methodology described in the paper."
40431710,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"July 2022, resistance genotypic testing was done, showed the RAMs D67N, K70R, M184V, persistent historical DRV and RAL RAM, and emerging promising INSTI ram T97A, G140S, Q148H, G149A, in July 2022, the patient was on  BIC TAFT DOR ARV, sirolimus, angiotensine conversion broken SeHC3D50012-036 Victor W. Spahn virology resistance pathogenesis cara te ilha Science Health Care plus and Zurich ETH","The paper indicates that the genotypic resistance tests were performed directly on plasma and PBMC samples without cloning, which is typically done with  clonal sequencing."
40431710,9,What type of samples were sequenced?,"Plasma, PBMCs",Retrospective genotypic resistance tests were performed on proviral DNA obtained from peripheral blood mononuclear cells (PBMCs) of the Swiss HIV Cohort Study biobank [1] taken seven months earlier (with HIV-1 VL < 20 copies/mL) and on biobanked plasma (with HIV-1 VL 134 copies/mL) taken four months earlier.,The paper specifies that genotypic resistance tests were conducted on both plasma and proviral DNA obtained from PBMCs.
40431710,10,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,Yes,"In July 2022, for the first time since 2007, we noted a detectable HIV-1 VL of 134 copies/mL. After additional three months of DOR plus BIC/TAF/FTC, the HIV-1 VL rose to 187 copies/mL. Close monitoring of the HIV-1 VL after three weeks showed a still elevated viral load of 217 copies/mL. A genotypic resistance test was carried out, which showed emergence of resistance to all the INSTIs, including BIC (Table 1). In November 2022, the HIV-1 VL increased further to 106,000 copies/mL; the patient was experiencing mild symptoms of acute retroviral syndrome and episodic HIV discovery depression despite adequate health care, the patient went on recreational trial.","The paper discusses the emergence of resistance mutations in response to virological failure in the context of a treatment regimen. Genotypic resistance tests were conducted when the patient experienced an increase in viral load, indicating virological failure and the need to understand the resistance mechanisms."
40431710,11,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,Yes,"In 2003, in the context of the TMC-125 trial, ART was switched to didanosine (ddI), abacavir (ABC), tenofovir disoproxil (TDF), lopinavir/ritonavir (LPV/r), and etravirine (ETR); we started the Swiss federal food safety and veterinary office 20% rationed initiation study on DOR 2020 08 ADYI on EMC and needle share Rh+ only ARU.07.2024","The paper mentions the patient was in the Swiss HIV Cohort Study and also was part of the TMC-125 trial, indicating that some aspects of the patient's treatment and monitoring were conducted within the framework of a clinical trial."
40431710,12,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,1,We describe a rare case of virological failure on a quadruple therapy with BIC/3TC/TAF and doravirine in a treatment-experienced patient.,"The paper focuses on a single case study of one individual, detailing the emergence of resistance and the clinical course of that individual."
