40763144,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,HIVDR data in the West-Nile region of Uganda across the 161 health facilities that offer comprehensive ART services,"The study involved collecting and analyzing HIVDR data from patient samples, indicating that sequences were obtained from patient samples."
40763144,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"We conducted a retrospective cross-sectional analysis of HIVDR data in the West-Nile region of Uganda across the 161 health facilities that offer comprehensive Anti-retroviral therapy (ART) services. All PLHIV, regardless of age, who had been on ART for at least one-year, experienced virological failure and underwent an HIVDR test between 1st January 2021–30th December 2023 were included in the study. Demographic and clinical data were extracted from the National HIVDR database.",The study focused on HIV drug resistance mutations and clinical data rather than in vitro drug susceptibility testing.
40763144,3,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,The GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates were not provided in the paper.,,"The paper does not mention any GenBank accession numbers, indicating that this information was not disclosed."
40763144,4,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,The paper does not specify which HIV genes were sequenced.,"RNA extraction is done using commercial kits followed by reverse transcription and Polymerase Chain Reaction (PCR) amplification. Sequencing, where individual mutations are identified using either Sanger sequencing or Next generation sequencing is then carried out. Mutations are identified and interpreted using the Stanford HIV drug resistance database.",The paper discusses the sequencing process but does not list specific genes that were sequenced.
40763144,5,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,Uganda,West-Nile region of Uganda across the 161 health facilities that offer comprehensive Anti-retroviral therapy (ART) services,"The samples were obtained from the West-Nile region of Uganda, as stated in the study."
40763144,6,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2021-2023,between 1st January 2021–30th December 2023 were included in the study.,"The study included samples collected between January 1, 2021, and December 30, 2023."
40763144,7,What method was used for sequencing?,The paper mentions the use of Sanger sequencing or Next Generation Sequencing.,"Here, RNA extraction is done using commercial kits followed by reverse transcription and Polymerase Chain Reaction (PCR) amplification. Sequencing, where individual mutations are identified using either Sanger sequencing or Next generation sequencing is then carried out.",The paper mentions these methods used for  sequencing.
40763144,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"HIVDR data in the West-Nile region of Uganda across the 161 health facilities that offer comprehensive ART services. All PLHIV, regardless of age, who had been on ART for at least one-year, experienced virological failure and underwent an HIVDR test between 1st January 2021–30th December 2023 were included in the study. Demographic and clinical data were extracted from the National HIVDR database. HIVDR was defined as having at least one mutation with a penalty score of ≥ 15. PLHIV were characterized based on age, gender and clinical history. Logistic regression models determined factors associated with HIVDR with a p-value of < 0.05 considered significant.All PLHIV on ART in Uganda receive a VL test annually. Those with a non-suppressed VL undergo intensive adherence counselling (IAC) for three months with a repeat VL at the end. Individuals with a persistent non- suppressed VL are eligible for an HIVDR test. A Plasma or dried blood spot (DBS) sample is collected and referred to the HIVDR testing facility at a central national laboratory. Here, RNA extraction is done using commercial kits followed by reverse transcription and Polymerase Chain Reaction (PCR) amplification. PCR is a molecular technique used to amplify specific DNA sequences by generating millions of copies from a small initial sample. This step is crucial for increasing the quantity of genetic material required for sequencing. Sequencing, where individual mutations are identified using either Sanger sequencing or Next generation sequencing is then carried out. Mutations are identified and interpreted using the Stanford HIV drug resistance database [18-20]. The results of the HIVDR test are uploaded onto the national database together with the patients clinical and demographic information.",The study didn't mention samples were cloned.
40763144,9,What type of samples were sequenced?,Plasma or dried blood spot (DBS) specimens,A Plasma or dried blood spot (DBS) sample is collected and referred to the HIVDR testing facility at a central national laboratory.,The paper states that either plasma or dried blood spot (DBS) specimens were collected for sequencing.
40763144,10,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,Yes,"The study included all PLHIV, regardless of age, who had been on ART for at least one year, had a non-suppressed HIV viral load (>1000 copies/ml) and underwent an HIVDR test between 1st January 2021–30th December 2023.",The study specifically focused on individuals with virological failure (non-suppressed viral load) on their treatment regimen.
40763144,11,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"We conducted a retrospective cross-sectional analysis of HIVDR data in the West-Nile region of Uganda across the 161 health facilities that offer comprehensive Anti-retroviral therapy (ART) services. All PLHIV, regardless of age, who had been on ART for at least one-year, experienced virological failure and underwent an HIVDR test between 1st January 2021–30th December 2023 were included in the study. Demographic and clinical data were extracted from the National HIVDR database. HIVDR was defined as having at least one mutation with a penalty score of ≥ 15. PLHIV were characterized based on age, gender and clinical history. Logistic regression models determined factors associated with HIVDR with a p-value of < 0.05 considered significant.","The study is a retrospective cross-sectional analysis, not a clinical trial."
40763144,12,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,295,"A total 295 records were analyzed. Of these, majority were female (56.6%) and adults aged ≥ 20 years (49.2%). The median age was 19 (Inter quartile range [IQR]: 13-41) years, and median duration on ART was 8 (IQR: 5-10) years. Overall, 218 (73.9%) had HIVDR with 66% of subjects having Non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase (NNRTI) mutations. M184V/I (50%), K103N (34%) and TAMS (26%) were the commonest mutations.",295 individuals had their samples obtained for HIV sequencing as stated in the results section.
40763144,13,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,"We conducted a retrospective cross-sectional analysis of HIVDR data in the West-Nile region of Uganda across the 161 health facilities that offer comprehensive Anti-retroviral therapy (ART) services. All PLHIV, regardless of age, who had been on ART for at least one-year, experienced virological failure and underwent an HIVDR test between 1st January 2021–30th December 2023 were included in the study. Demographic and clinical data were extracted from the National HIVDR database. HIVDR was defined as having at least one mutation with a penalty score of ≥ 15. PLHIV were characterized based on age, gender and clinical history. Logistic regression models determined factors associated with HIVDR with a p-value of < 0.05 considered significant.","The study included individuals who had been on ART, indicating they had previously received ARV drugs."
