40839365,7,What method was used for sequencing?,Sanger sequencing,"HIV RNA was extracted using NUCLISENS easyMAG (BioMerieux) from 200 μL of plasma, with 10 μL of extracted RNA; gene fragments of protease and reverse transcriptase and integrase were amplified and sequenced using HIV-1 Genotyping kit with Integrase and the 3500XL Genetic Analyzers (Applied Biosystems).",The paper describes the use of Sanger sequencing for genotypic analysis.
40839365,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"Hiv Rna was extracted using nuclisens easymag (biomerieux) from 200 μl of plasma, with 10 μl of extracted rna; gene fragments of protease and reverse transcriptase and integrase were amplified and sequenced using hiv-1 genotyping kit with integrase and the 3500xl genetic analyzers (applied biosystems)",The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing.
40839365,9,What type of samples were sequenced?,Plasma,"We enrolled consenting adults (≥ 15 years), on DBR for ≥ 9 months, when returning for follow-up VL testing after a previous high VL (≥ 1000 copies/mL) and completing IAC. Enrolled PLHIV submitted plasma samples for VL testing and those with VL ≥ 1000 copies/mL (confirmed virologic failure) underwent genotypic HIVDR testing.",The paper specifies that plasma samples were used for genotypic analysis.
40839365,10,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,Yes,"Among 213 participants with confirmed virologic failure, 212 samples were successfully genotyped for protease, reverse transcriptase, and integrase.",The paper reports that sequences were obtained from individuals with confirmed virologic failure.
40839365,11,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,A national HIV drug resistance (HIVDR) survey in adults on DBR with high viral load (VL) results after intensive adherence counseling (IAC).,"The study was a national survey, not a clinical trial."
40839365,12,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,213,"Among 213 participants with confirmed virologic failure, 212 samples were successfully genotyped for protease, reverse transcriptase, and integrase.",The paper states that samples from 213 participants with confirmed virologic failure were obtained for sequencing.
40839365,13,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,"The prevalence of drug resistance mutations (DRMs) to nucleotide reverse-transcriptase inhibitors was 22.6% (95% CI: 17.4% to 27.8%), to non-nucleoside reverse-transcriptase inhibitors 55.1% (95% CI 48.2% to 62.1%), and to protease inhibitors 4.9% (95% CI 1.3% to 8.5%). Prevalence of major dolutegravir DRMs was 8.5% (95% CI 5.3% to 11.9%).","The paper reports drug resistance mutations in individuals on a dolutegravir-based regimen, indicating they had received ARV drugs."
40839365,14,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTIs, NNRTIs, PIs, INSTIs","Prevalence of drug resistance mutations (DRMs) to nucleotide reverse-transcriptase inhibitors was 22.6% (95% CI: 17.4% to 27.8%), to non-nucleoside reverse-transcriptase inhibitors 55.1% (95% CI 48.2% to 62.1%), and to protease inhibitors 4.9% (95% CI 1.3% to 8.5%), Major INSTI associated DRM to DTG was 8.6% (5.3%-11.9%)","The paper reports mutations associated with resistance to various drug classes, including NRTIs, NNRTIs, PIs, and INSTIs."
40839365,15,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"Abacavir, emtricitabine, lamivudine, tenofovir disoproxil fumarate, efavirenz, nevirapine, dolutegravir","For resistance to individual NRTIs, prevalence of low, intermediate, or high resistance was most common for abacavir (20.1%) followed by emtricitabine (18.2%), lamivudine (16.9%), stavudine (12.8%), and tenofovir disoproxil fumarate (10.3%). For individual NNRTIs, low, intermediate, or high resistance to efavirenz and nevirapine was equally high (49.5%). For INSTIs, resistance to cabotegravir was 9.4% and to DTG was 8.5% (95% CI: 5.3% to 11.9%).","The paper lists specific drugs for which resistance mutations were identified, indicating these were received before sequencing."
40839365,16,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,No GenBank accession numbers are provided for sequenced HIV isolates in the paper.,The paper does not provide any GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced isolates.
40857111,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,"Yes, this analysis reports HIV-1 resistance results to the end of masked treatment (DTG+F/TDF, n = 122) and to study end (B/F/TAF, n = 119; DTG+F/TDF to B/F/TAF, n = 89).","This analysis reports HIV-1 resistance results to the end of masked treatment (DTG+F/TDF, n = 122) and to study end (B/F/TAF, n = 119; DTG+F/TDF to B/F/TAF, n = 89).","The paper includes data on HIV-1 resistance, which indicates that HIV sequences were obtained from patient samples."
40857111,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,"No, the study specifically focuses on genotypic resistance and virologic outcomes rather than in vitro drug susceptibility.","Baseline resistance was determined using historical and screening genotypes of protease (PR), reverse transcriptase (RT), and integrase (IN), if available; Participants with virologic failure had postbaseline resistance testing of PR/RT/IN; those subsequently not achieving virologic suppression (VS; HIV-1 RNA < 50 copies/mL) were included in final resistance analysis population (RAP).",The paper focuses on genotypic resistance data from patient samples and does not mention in vitro drug susceptibility testing or results.
40857111,3,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,The paper does not provide specific GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates.,No evidence,The paper does not mention any GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates.
