41004608,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"Between 2015 and 2021, human immunodeficiency virus type 1 (HIV-1) whole genome sequencing was performed on samples from people with recently acquired HIV-1 in the United Kingdom.",The study involved sequencing HIV-1 genomes from patient samples to analyze the prevalence of nonintegrase INSTI resistance-associated mutations.
41004608,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"The presence of clinically relevant drug resistance mutations (surveillance drug resistance mutations [SDRMs]) was identified by submitting sequences to the Stanford Calibrated Population Resistance tool [15].  Overall, Env Y61H (33 [10%]), A539V (16 [5.0%]), 3'PPT c9053t (17 [5.0%]), and NC N8S (16 [4.8%]) were the most prevalent nonintegrase enzyme mutations.",The study focused on sequencing data and clinical outcomes rather than reporting in vitro drug susceptibility assays.
41004608,3,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,The paper does not provide any GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates. The complete genomic sequencing of HIV was performed using a previously described sequence capture method and the short read data were assembled into consensus whole genomes.,No GenBank accession numbers were provided in the text.,The publication did not mention any specific GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced isolates.
41004608,4,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,"The paper reports sequencing of the whole genome of HIV-1, including nonintegrase domains.",Human immunodeficiency virus type 1 (HIV-1) whole genome sequencing was performed on samples from people with recently acquired HIV-1 in the United Kingdom.,"The study involved whole genome sequencing, which includes all the key viral genes."
41004608,5,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,United Kingdom,"Between 2015 and 2021, human immunodeficiency virus type 1 (HIV-1) whole genome sequencing was performed on samples from people with recently acquired HIV-1 in the United Kingdom.",The study specifically mentioned that the samples were from individuals in the United Kingdom.
