41023944,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"Resistance mutations were identified via Sanger sequencing and interpreted using the Stanford HIV Drug Resistance Database. Plasma samples from participants with confirmed virological failure were collected and sent to the Zimpeto DREAM laboratory, where viral RNA was extracted and amplified using an in-house reverse transcription and nested PCR method. Sequencing of the HIV integrase region was performed using the Sanger method at INQABA Biotechnical Industries, South Africa.",The paper mentions that resistance mutations were identified via Sanger sequencing and that plasma samples were collected from participants with confirmed virological failure for sequencing purposes.
41023944,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"Methods We conducted a retrospective observational study in five DREAM centers in Mozambique. HIV-positive individuals on DTG-based antiretroviral therapy (ART) with confirmed virological failure (HIV RNA  >1000 copies/mL) between July 2022 and December 2024 were included. Participants underwent genotypic resistance testing after six months of enhanced adherence support. Resistance mutations were identified via Sanger sequencing and interpreted using the Stanford HIV Drug Resistance Database.  Results Of 28 individual tested, 13 (46.4%) exhibited DTG resistance (12/13 with intermediate or high-level resistance); 92.3% harboured HIV-1 subtype C. Frequently observed mutations included G118R (43.8%), E138K (43.8%), L74M (31.3%), and R263K (25.0%), often in combination. All DTG-resistant individuals who underwent testing for other drug classes (n = 9) also showed co-resistance to NRTIs and/or NNRTIs. Notably, high-level resistance emerged also in 6 failing participants having shifted to DTG while virologically suppressed.",The paper focuses on genotypic resistance testing and does not mention any in vitro drug susceptibility assays or data.
41023944,3,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,No accession numbers reported,"Resistance mutations were identified via Sanger sequencing and interpreted using the Stanford HIV Drug Resistance Database. Plasma samples from participants with confirmed virological failure were collected and sent to the Zimpeto DREAM laboratory, where viral RNA was extracted and amplified using an in-house reverse transcription and nested PCR method. Sequencing of the HIV integrase region was performed using the Sanger method at INQABA Biotechnical Industries, South Africa.",The paper does not provide any GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates.
41023944,4,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,"HIV integrase, reverse transcriptase, protease","Resistance mutations were identified via Sanger sequencing and interpreted using the Stanford HIV Drug Resistance Database. Plasma samples from participants with confirmed virological failure were collected and sent to the Zimpeto DREAM laboratory, where viral RNA was extracted and amplified using an in-house reverse transcription and nested PCR method. Sequencing of the HIV integrase region was performed using the Sanger method at INQABA Biotechnical Industries, South Africa. Sequences were analyzed and interpreted directly at INQABA using the Stanford HIV Drug Resistance Database. DTG resistance was classified according to Stanford penalty scores as follows: susceptible (score < 10)(S), low-level resistance (10-14) (LLR), intermediate resistance (15-29) (IR), and high-level resistance (≥ 30) (HLR). Resistance mutations were identified in the integrase region, and, when available, in the reverse transcriptase and protease regions.","The paper reports sequencing of the integrase, reverse transcriptase and protease regions for resistance testing."
41023944,5,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,Mozambique,We conducted a retrospective observational study in five DREAM centers in Mozambique. HIV-positive individuals on DTG-based antiretroviral therapy (ART) with confirmed virological failure (HIV RNA  >1000 copies/mL) between July 2022 and December 2024 were included.,The study was conducted across five centers of the DREAM program in Mozambique.
41023944,6,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2023 to 2024,"The current analysis builds on a previously published cohort [9], expanding the sample by including all eligible participants who underwent genotypic resistance testing due to confirmed virological failure, even after an adherence reinforce intervention, while on DTG-based ART between July 2022 and December 2024. The study was approved by the National Bioethics Committee for Health of Mozambique (Ref: 430/CNBS/24, July 19, 2024).","The samples were obtained between 2022 and 2024, as mentioned in the study methods."
41023944,7,What method was used for sequencing?,Sanger sequencing,"Plasma samples from participants with confirmed virological failure were collected and sent to the Zimpeto DREAM laboratory, where viral RNA was extracted and amplified using an in-house reverse transcription and nested PCR method. Sequencing of the HIV integrase region was performed using the Sanger method at INQABA Biotechnical Industries, South Africa.",The paper specifies that Sanger sequencing was used for the analysis of the HIV integrase region.
41023944,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"Resistance mutations were identified via Sanger sequencing and interpreted using the Stanford HIV Drug Resistance Database. Plasma samples from participants with confirmed virological failure were collected and sent to the Zimpeto DREAM laboratory, where viral RNA was extracted and amplified using an in-house reverse transcription and nested PCR method. Sequencing of the HIV integrase region was performed using the Sanger method at INQABA Biotechnical Industries, South Africa. Sequences were analyzed and interpreted directly at INQABA using the Stanford HIV Drug Resistance Database. DTG resistance was classified according to Stanford penalty scores as follows: susceptible (score < 10)(S), low-level resistance (10-14) (LLR), intermediate resistance (15-29) (IR), and high-level resistance (≥ 30) (HLR). Resistance mutations were identified in the integrase region, and, when available, in the reverse transcriptase and protease regions.",The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing.
41023944,9,What type of samples were sequenced?,Plasma samples,"Plasma samples from participants with confirmed virological failure were collected and sent to the Zimpeto DREAM laboratory, where viral RNA was extracted and amplified using an in-house reverse transcription and nested PCR method. Sequencing of the HIV integrase region was performed using the Sanger method at INQABA Biotechnical Industries, South Africa.",The paper states that plasma samples from participants were used for sequencing.
41023944,10,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,Yes,"We included HIV-positive individuals on dolutegravir (DTG)-based antiretroviral therapy (ART) who experienced virological failure, defined as HIV RNA >1000 copies/mL, between June 1, 2023, and December 31, 2024. In the considered period, 28 individuals on DTG-based ART who underwent resistance testing following confirmed virological failure (HIV RNA ≥ 1000 copies/mL) after six months of enhanced adherence support were included in the analysis.",The study included individuals on DTG-based ART with confirmed virological failure.
41023944,11,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,No,"This is a retrospective observational study conducted across five centers of the DREAM program in Mozambique, including two centers in Maputo City, one in Maputo Province, and two in Sofala Province. The DREAM program is a community-based, primary care HIV initiative that integrates clinical follow-up, laboratory monitoring, and social support, and has been active in Mozambique since 2002.  The study was approved by the National Bioethics Committee for Health of Mozambique (Ref: 430/CNBS/24, July 19, 2024).","The study is a retrospective observational study, not a clinical trial."
41023944,12,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,28,"In the considered period, 28 individuals on DTG-based ART who underwent resistance testing following confirmed virological failure (HIV RNA ≥ 1000 copies/mL) after six months of enhanced adherence support were included in the analysis.",The paper states that 28 individuals underwent resistance testing.
41023944,13,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,"We included HIV-positive individuals on dolutegravir (DTG)-based antiretroviral therapy (ART) who experienced virological failure, defined as HIV RNA  >1000 copies/mL, between June 1, 2023, and December 31, 2024. We conducted a retrospective observational study in five DREAM centers in Mozambique. The current analysis builds on a previously published cohort [9], expanding the sample by including all eligible participants who underwent genotypic resistance testing due to confirmed virological failure, even after an adherence reinforce intervention, while on DTG-based ART between July 2022 and December 2024. We also reported all the participants were on the second line or thirs line treatment: the most recent ART regimen included second-line (n = 15) or third-line (n = 3)DTG-based regimens; four had not received any ART, most of them ineligible for ART initiation due to active opportunistic infections (OIs), leading to resultant disitinct resistance profiles",The paper mentioned sequences were from individual who had received ARV drugs.
41023944,14,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTI,NNRTI,PI,II","We included HIV-positive individuals on dolutegravir (DTG)-based antiretroviral therapy (ART) who experienced virological failure, defined as HIV RNA  >1000 copies/mL, between June 1, 2023, and December 31, 2024. We conducted a retrospective observational study in five DREAM centers in Mozambique. The current analysis builds on a previously published cohort [9], expanding the sample by including all eligible participants who underwent genotypic resistance testing due to confirmed virological failure, even after an adherence reinforce intervention, while on DTG-based ART between July 2022 and December 2024. We also reported all the participants were on the second line or thirs line treatment: the most recent ART regimen included second-line (n = 15) or third-line (n = 3)DTG-based regimens; four had not received any ART, most of them ineligible for ART initiation due to active opportunistic infections (OIs), leading to resultant disitinct resistance profiles","The paper mentions the most recent ART regimen included second-line (n = 15) or third-line (n = 3)DTG-based regimens; four had not received any ART, most of them ineligible for ART initiation due to active opportunistic infections (OIs), leading to resultant disitinct resistance profiles."
41023944,15,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,No specific drugs,"We included HIV-positive individuals on dolutegravir (DTG)-based antiretroviral therapy (ART) who experienced virological failure, defined as HIV RNA  >1000 copies/mL, between June 1, 2023, and December 31, 2024. We conducted a retrospective observational study in five DREAM centers in Mozambique. The current analysis builds on a previously published cohort [9], expanding the sample by including all eligible participants who underwent genotypic resistance testing due to confirmed virological failure, even after an adherence reinforce intervention, while on DTG-based ART between July 2022 and December 2024. We also reported all the participants were on the second line or thirs line treatment: the most recent ART regimen included second-line (n = 15) or third-line (n = 3)DTG-based regimens; four had not received any ART, most of them ineligible for ART initiation due to active opportunistic infections (OIs), leading to resultant disitinct resistance profiles",The paper does not provide detailed information about the specific drugs received by individuals before sample sequencing.
41023944,16,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No evidence,"Resistance mutations were identified via Sanger sequencing and interpreted using the Stanford HIV Drug Resistance Database. Plasma samples from participants with confirmed virological failure were collected and sent to the Zimpeto DREAM laboratory, where viral RNA was extracted and amplified using an in-house reverse transcription and nested PCR method. Sequencing of the HIV integrase region was performed using the Sanger method at INQABA Biotechnical Industries, South Africa. Sequences were analyzed and interpreted directly at INQABA using the Stanford HIV Drug Resistance Database. DTG resistance was classified according to Stanford penalty scores as follows: susceptible (score < 10)(S), low-level resistance (10-14) (LLR), intermediate resistance (15-29) (IR), and high-level resistance (≥ 30) (HLR). Resistance mutations were identified in the integrase region, and, when available, in the reverse transcriptase and protease regions.",The paper does not mention any publicly available sequences.
41029850,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,"Yes, individual samples with confirmed virological failure were sequenced in HIV-1 protease and reverse-transcriptase genes using Sanger sequencing.",Individual samples with confirmed virological failure were sequenced in HIV-1 protease and reverse-transcriptase genes using Sanger sequencing and analysed using Stanford HIVdatabase.v.9.4.,"The methodology section explicitly states that individual patient samples were sequenced, indicating that the study involved HIV sequences from patient samples."
41029850,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,"No, the paper does not report in vitro drug susceptibility data.","This study describes patterns of acquired drug resistance (ADR) and potentially active drugs for subsequent ART regimens in individuals failing treatment in Cameroon. We conducted a cross-sectional, laboratory-based sentinel study among ART-failing individuals from October 2022 through April 2023 at the Chantal Biya International Reference Centre, Yaoundé-Cameroon. Individual samples with confirmed virological failure were sequenced in HIV-1 protease and reverse-transcriptase genes using Sanger sequencing and analysed using Stanford HIVdatabase.v.9.4. Overall, 203 individuals were enrolled, median [IQR] age 37 [16-47] years with 58.1% (118/203) being female. Median [IQR] duration on ART was 10 [6.9-13.3] years, with majority failing second-line (78.7%; 160/203). HIVDR rate was 85.3% (29/34) and 88.1% (140/160) in those failing first-line and second-line therapy respectively (p = 0.7). NNRTI, NRTI and PI/r resistance in individuals failing first-line ART was 85.3% (9/34), 82.4% (28/34) and 2.9% (1/34) respectively. In second-line failure, NNRTI, NRTI and PI/r resistance was 86.8% (138/160), 84.9% (135/160) and 47.17% (75/160) respectively. NRTI-NNRTI dual-class-resistance was 82.4% (28/34) after first-line and 84.3% (134/160) after second-line (p = 0.842), while triple-class-resistance was 2.9% (1/34) after first-line and 45.3% (72/160) after second-line (p < 0.0001). After first-line, TDF and AZT maintained potential efficacy in respectively 53.4% (18/34) and 46.7% (16/34) of individuals, while 46.7% (16/34) had cross-resistance to second-generation NNRTI. After second-line failure, 95.6% (153/160) maintained DRV/r efficacy, as compared to 67.3% for ATV/r (p < 0.0002), 73.9% for LPV/r (p < 0.0002), and 34.7% (56/160) for RPV/DOR.","The study focuses on genotypic resistance testing and the sequencing of HIV-1 genes from patient samples, not on in vitro drug susceptibility testing."
41029850,3,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,"PQ893197, PQ893192, OQ985810, OR259747, PQ893191","However, sequences have been submitted to Genebank with available accession numbers provided (PQ893197, PQ893192, OQ985810, OR259747 and PQ893191) in the current version of the manuscript while the rest will be completed when made available by Genbank (submission ID: 2961867)",The sequences are mentioned to be submitted to Genbank with specific accession numbers provided in the supplementary information.
41029850,4,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,The paper reports sequencing of the protease (PR) and reverse transcriptase (RT) genes of HIV-1.,"GRT was performed in the pol gene (protease (PR) and reverse transcriptase (RT)) on the stored samples with plasma viral load 8600 copies/ml, using the previously validated inhouse protocol as described by Fokam et al. [21].","The genotypic resistance testing (GRT) was performed in the pol gene, which includes the protease and reverse transcriptase genes."
41029850,5,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,The sequenced samples were obtained from Cameroon.,HIV-1 drug resistance (HIVDR) surveillance among individuals failing antiretroviral therapy (ART) is essential to selecting optimal ART-combinations for use in a public health approach in low-middle-income countries (LMICs) where routine HIVDR-testing remains limited. This study describes patterns of acquired drug resistance (ADR) and potentially active drugs for subsequent ART regimens in individuals failing treatment in Cameroon.,"The study was conducted at the 'Chantal Biya' International Reference Centre in Yaoundé, Cameroon, and it focused on individuals failing antiretroviral therapy in Cameroon."
41029850,6,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,The samples were obtained from October 2022 through April 2023.,"We conducted a cross-sectional, laboratory-based sentinel study among ART-failing individuals from October 2022 through April 2023 at the Chantal Biya International Reference Centre, Yaoundé-Cameroon.",The study mentions the specific timeframe during which the samples were collected.
41029850,7,What method was used for sequencing?,The sequencing method used was Sanger sequencing.,Individual samples with confirmed virological failure were sequenced in HIV-1 protease and reverse-transcriptase genes using Sanger sequencing.,The methodology section explicitly states that Sanger sequencing was used for sequencing the HIV-1 genes.
41029850,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,"No, the paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing.",Not applicable,"The methodology does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing, indicating that the samples were directly sequenced."
41029850,9,What type of samples were sequenced?,The type of samples sequenced were individual plasma samples from blood.,"Among ART-failing individuals in Cameroon, levels of ADR are high with significant levels of cross-resistance to second-generation NNRTI, hence potentially jeopardizing the use of long-acting cabotegravir/rilpivirine. Furthermore, HIVDR testing could be considered from first-line failure in such settings, but chiefly following second-line failure wherein triple-class-resistance is common and calls for novel antiretrovirals in similar LMICs","The study focuses on individuals failing antiretroviral therapy and mentions plasma viral load, which indicates that plasma samples were sequenced."
41029850,10,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,"Yes, sequences were obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen.",Participants were on documented ART for at least 12 months and experiencing virological failure (viral load 8600 copies/ml).,The study mentioned that the samples were from individuals experiencing virological failure on their treatment regimens.
41029850,11,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,"No, the paper does not indicate that the patients were in a clinical trial.","We conducted a cross-sectional, laboratory-based sentinel study among ART-failing individuals from October 2022 through April 2023 at the Chantal Biya International Reference Centre, Yaoundé-Cameroon. Individual samples with confirmed virological failure were sequenced in HIV-1 protease and reverse-transcriptase genes using Sanger sequencing and analysed using Stanford HIVdatabase.v.9.4. Overall, 203 individuals were enrolled, median [IQR] age 37 [16-47] years with 58.1% (118/203) being female. Median [IQR] duration on ART was 10 [6.9-13.3] years, with majority failing second-line (78.7%; 160/203). HIVDR rate was 85.3% (29/34) and 88.1% (140/160) in those failing first-line and second-line therapy respectively (p = 0.7). NNRTI, NRTI and PI/r resistance in individuals failing first-line ART was 85.3% (9/34), 82.4% (28/34) and 2.9% (1/34) respectively. In second-line failure, NNRTI, NRTI and PI/r resistance was 86.8% (138/160), 84.9% (135/160) and 47.17% (75/160) respectively. NRTI-NNRTI dual-class-resistance was 82.4% (28/34) after first-line and 84.3% (134/160) after second-line (p = 0.842), while triple-class-resistance was 2.9% (1/34) after first-line and 45.3% (72/160) after second-line (p < 0.0001). After first-line, TDF and AZT maintained potential efficacy in respectively 53.4% (18/34) and 46.7% (16/34) of individuals, while 46.7% (16/34) had cross-resistance to second-generation NNRTI. After second-line failure, 95.6% (153/160) maintained DRV/r efficacy, as compared to 67.3% for ATV/r (p < 0.0002), 73.9% for LPV/r (p < 0.0002), and 34.7% (56/160) for RPV/DOR.","The study was a cross-sectional, laboratory-based sentinel study focused on ART-failing individuals, not a clinical trial."
41029850,12,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,203 individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing.,"Overall, 203 individuals were enrolled, median age 37 [16-47] years with 58.1% (118/203) being female.",The study included 203 individuals who met the inclusion criteria and had their samples sequenced.
