41129268,16,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,No evidence,The paper does not mention that the sequences were made publicly available.
41130593,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,Yes,"Resistance analyses from P1 and P2, which provide the first data regarding emergent drug resistance in the context of LEN for PrEP. Upon HIV-1 diagnosis, plasma samples were collected for HIV-1 RNA quantification and resistance analyses.","The paper describes resistance analyses from the PURPOSE 1 and 2 studies, indicating that HIV sequences were obtained from patient samples for genotyping resistance mutations."
41130593,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"The article focuses on resistance analyses and the emergence of drug resistance mutations in patient samples during the PURPOSE trials, rather than reporting in vitro susceptibility data.","The content of the paper does not mention any in vitro drug susceptibility testing or results, indicating that it does not report such data."
41130593,3,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,No data reported,,"The paper does not provide any GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates, as it focuses on resistance data rather than sequence accession numbers."
41130593,4,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,"HIV-1 capsid, protease, reverse transcriptase, and integrase genes","Participants who acquired HIV-1 with a viral load of ≥ 200 copies/mL were evaluated for resistance by genotyping of the HIV-1 capsid, protease, reverse transcriptase and integrase genes at HIV diagnosis.","The paper states that genotyping of the HIV-1 capsid, protease, reverse transcriptase, and integrase genes was conducted for resistance analyses."
41130593,5,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,No data reported,,"The paper does not specify the countries from which the sequenced samples were obtained, focusing instead on the study populations."
41130593,6,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2021-2023,August 2021–September 2023; June 2021–December 2023,"The study was conducted over several years, from 2021 to 2023, as indicated in the data availability statement."
41130593,7,What method was used for sequencing?,Next-generation sequencing,"Genotypic CA resistance testing at Monogram was conducted using a next-generation sequencing platform (MiSeq; Illumina), with reporting of mutants present in ≥10% of sequences obtained for a given sample.",The paper mentions that next-generation sequencing was used for genotypic resistance testing.
41130593,8,Were samples cloned prior to sequencing?,No,"The paper does not mention any cloning of samples prior to sequencing, indicating that cloning was not performed.",The content of the paper does not provide any information regarding the cloning of samples.
41130593,9,What type of samples were sequenced?,Plasma samples,"Upon HIV-1 diagnosis, plasma samples were collected for HIV-1 RNA quantification and resistance analyses.",The paper indicates that plasma samples were collected and analyzed for resistance.
41130593,10,Were any sequences obtained from individuals with virological failure on a treatment regimen?,Yes,Virologic suppression (HIV-1 RNA < 50 copies/mL) was achieved on dolutegravir (DTG)/lamivudine (3TC)/TDF within 9 weeks of HIV-1 acquisition (Supplementary Table 3).,"The paper mentions resistance in individuals receiving treatment, indicating some had virologic failure or resistance."
41130593,11,Were the patients in the study in a clinical trial?,Yes,"The study was part of the PURPOSE 1 and 2 trials, which were clinical trials assessing the efficacy and safety of LEN and other PrEP agents.",The paper describes the study participants as part of clinical trials (PURPOSE 1 and 2).
41130593,12,How many individuals had samples obtained for HIV sequencing?,55 in P1 and 11 in P2,"Of the 5338 participants in the P1 EAP, 55 acquired HIV-1 after Day 1 (at primary analysis; Table 1) [8]. Of the 3265 participants in the P2 EAP, 11 acquired HIV-1 after Day 1.",The paper provides data on the number of individuals from whom samples were obtained for sequencing.
41130593,13,Does the paper report HIV sequences from individuals who had previously received ARV drugs?,Yes,"In P1, 2/37 participants analyzed for resistance in the F/TAF group had M184I  ␀b1  K65R. In the F/TDF groups, M184M/I/V was detected in 1/16 participants (P1) and 1/9 participants (P2). In P2, one participant developed M184V; all these mutations indicate drug resistance, meaning that sequences are form RAV respecting the drug they received beforehand","The paper mentions sequences from individuals who had received drugs, some mutations are RAV which is expected sequences from those who have been previously treated."
41130593,14,Which drug classes were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"NRTIs, INSTIs","In P1, 2/37 participants analyzed for resistance in the F/TAF group had M184I ␀b1 K65R. In the F/TDF groups, M184M/I/V was detected in 1/16 participants (P1) and 1/9 participants (P2). In P2, one participant developed M184V; all these mutations indicate drug resistance, meaning that sequences are form RAV respecting the drug they received beforehand","The paper mentions participants received NRTI, INSTI drugs which belong to Anti retro viral drugs."
41130593,15,Which drugs were received by individuals in the study before sample sequencing?,"Emtricitabine, Tenofovir DF, Tenofovir alafenamide","Emtricitabine/tenofovir alafenamide (F/TAF) and emtricitabine/tenofovir disoproxil fumarate (F/TDF) were evaluated in the PURPOSE trials, and participants received these drugs as part of the study protocol for PrEP and resistance analyses.",The paper mentions specific drugs that were part of the study protocol.
41130593,16,Were sequences from the paper made publicly available?,No,"The paper does not provide any GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates, as it focuses on resistance data rather than sequence accession numbers.",The paper does not mention any public availability of the sequences.
41140464,1,Does the paper report HIV sequences from patient samples?,No,"Surveillance of HIV-1 molecular diversity and drug resistance mutations (DRMs) is a linchpin for monitoring viral evolution and treatment efficiency. However, consolidated reports on the same are limited, and therefore, the pursuit of meta-analysis was sought to analyze genetic diversity and drug resistance patterns of HIV-1 pol gene and their geographical distributions in four East African countries (Kenya, Uganda, Tanzania, and Ethiopia). We retrieved 7614 HIV-1 pol gene sequences, deposited between 2015 and 2025 from the Los Alamos HIV databases.","The paper does not mention that the HIV sequences were obtained from patient samples. Instead, it describes the sequences as being retrieved from a public database (Los Alamos HIV Database). This indicates that the sequences are not directly from patient samples but rather from a database."
41140464,2,Does the paper report in vitro drug susceptibility data?,No,"Surveillance of HIV-1 molecular diversity and drug resistance mutations (DRMs) is a linchpin for monitoring viral evolution and treatment efficiency. However, consolidated reports on the same are limited, and therefore, the pursuit of meta-analysis was sought to analyze genetic diversity and drug resistance patterns of HIV-1 pol gene and their geographical distributions in four East African countries (Kenya, Uganda, Tanzania, and Ethiopia). We retrieved 7614 HIV-1 pol gene sequences, deposited between 2015 and 2025 from the Los Alamos HIV databases. The predominant HIV-1 subtypes were A1 (40.2%), C (21.5%), and D (17.7%), with geographical variability. A notable frequency of inter-subtype recombinant was observed with recombinants A1D (9.5%) and A1C (2.94%) being prevalent. Few CRFs (>0.1%) were identified. DRM were present in 42.8% of the sequences, with the majority associated with NNRTIs (36.5%) and NRTIs (25.5%). The most frequently associated mutations were K103N and M184V. Although resistance to INSTI (3.7%) remained minimal, its presence warrants continued monitoring. A significant association between HIV-1 subtypes and DRM prevalence was observed (χ2=102.43,p<0.0001), with subtypes showing varied resistance burdens. These findings underscore the variability in HIV-1 genetic diversity across studied East African countries, highlighting the need for region-specific interventions, to optimize HIV-1 control in this region.","The paper focuses on genetic diversity and drug resistance patterns of HIV-1 sequences obtained from a database, and there is no mention of in vitro drug susceptibility testing or data."
41140464,3,What were the GenBank accession numbers for sequenced HIV isolates?,No,No evidence,"The paper does not provide any GenBank accession numbers for the sequenced HIV isolates, as it focuses on data obtained from the Los Alamos National Laboratory database."
41140464,4,Which HIV genes were reported to have been sequenced?,"PR, RT, IN","A total of 7724 HIV-1 pol gene sequences were initially retrieved from LANL HIV sequence database, comprising sequence collected and deposited between 2015 and 2025. Following quality filtering which involved the removal of duplicate entries and sequences with high levels of ambiguous nucleotide characters, 110 sequences were excluded, resulting in 7614 sequences for downstream analysis. These included 5730 PR/RT, 734 RT, and 1594 IN sequences.","The paper mentions the sequencing of the PR (Protease), RT (Reverse Transcriptase), and IN (Integrase) regions of the HIV-1 pol gene."
41140464,5,From which countries were the sequenced samples obtained?,"Kenya, Uganda, Tanzania, Ethiopia","We retrieved 7614 HIV-1 pol gene sequences, deposited between 2015 and 2025 from the Los Alamos HIV databases. The sequences were sourced from four EA countries: Kenya, Uganda, Tanzania, and Ethiopia. The distribution of HIV-1 pol gene sequences by country is presented in Table 1.","The paper states that the sequences were retrieved from four East African countries: Kenya, Uganda, Tanzania, and Ethiopia."
41140464,6,From what years were the sequenced samples obtained?,2015-2025,"In June 2025, HIV-1 pol gene sequences deposited between 2015 and 2025 from four East African countries were retrieved in FASTA format with subtyping information from the Los Alamos National Laboratory (LANL) HIV Database (https://www.hiv.lanl.gov).",The paper specifies that the sequences were deposited between 2015 and 2025.
